1
00:00:02,590 --> 00:00:03,550
Hello, English learners.

2
00:00:03,920 --> 00:00:05,010
Welcome back to EnglishPod.

3
00:00:05,360 --> 00:00:06,360
My name is Marco.

4
00:00:06,360 --> 00:00:06,990
And my name is Catherine.

5
00:00:07,440 --> 00:00:10,160
And today we're talking about being on the road.

6
00:00:10,350 --> 00:00:14,910
Yeah, we're going to be on the road, but we're going to encounter a little bit of a car problems.

7
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:16,750
My favorite kind.

8
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Yeah.

9
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:18,530
I think it's happened to everyone.

10
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:23,870
You're on the road or maybe you're coming back from work, you get a flat tire or something happens to your car, right?

11
00:00:24,050 --> 00:00:25,390
And it's always when you're in a rush.

12
00:00:26,440 --> 00:00:27,530
And most of the time it's raining.

13
00:00:28,200 --> 00:00:29,200
Yeah.

14
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:30,200
It's snowing.

15
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:33,760
So all of the circumstances definitely, well, they don't help.

16
00:00:33,760 --> 00:00:34,050
Let's just say.

17
00:00:34,760 --> 00:00:35,760
All right.

18
00:00:35,760 --> 00:00:41,560
So before we listen to this dialogue, let's take a look at some of the words in today's vocabulary preview.

19
00:00:41,560 --> 00:00:42,560
Vocabulary preview.

20
00:00:42,890 --> 00:00:43,410
All right.

21
00:00:43,640 --> 00:00:45,960
So we're going to take a look at two words.

22
00:00:45,960 --> 00:00:46,960
They're kind of strange.

23
00:00:47,130 --> 00:00:49,150
I don't know, kind of difficult to understand.

24
00:00:49,560 --> 00:00:52,040
So that's what we're going to explain them.

25
00:00:52,040 --> 00:00:54,130
The first one is spew, to spew.

26
00:00:54,720 --> 00:00:55,390
It's a verb, okay?

27
00:00:55,880 --> 00:00:56,880
To spew.

28
00:00:56,880 --> 00:00:59,000
Well, what can spew?

29
00:00:59,000 --> 00:00:59,570
Volcano can spew.

30
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Exactly.

31
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:02,240
An engine can spew.

32
00:01:02,240 --> 00:01:03,440
A person can spew.

33
00:01:03,440 --> 00:01:04,440
Yeah.

34
00:01:04,440 --> 00:01:04,710
That's not very nice.

35
00:01:05,440 --> 00:01:06,440
No.

36
00:01:06,440 --> 00:01:12,830
So basically it's to like expel or to shoot out from your mouth or something liquid.

37
00:01:13,300 --> 00:01:14,510
So in the case of a person, right?

38
00:01:14,850 --> 00:01:18,670
So if a person spews, it means a person throws up, but it's usually very forceful.

39
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:19,800
All right.

40
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:20,270
It's very messy.

41
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:23,200
A volcano can spew lava.

42
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:23,330
Exactly.

43
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:25,890
So a volcano is spewing lava.

44
00:01:26,800 --> 00:01:32,250
Now for the next word that we have, it's an informal way of saying that something is broken.

45
00:01:32,360 --> 00:01:34,360
You could say it's busted.

46
00:01:34,360 --> 00:01:34,630
It's busted.

47
00:01:35,020 --> 00:01:36,510
My DVD player is busted.

48
00:01:36,760 --> 00:01:38,010
Man, my phone is busted.

49
00:01:38,490 --> 00:01:44,850
So it's an informal way, very popular, very common I would say, to describe that something is not working.

50
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:48,880
I don't think many people actually say, well, it's broken or it's damaged.

51
00:01:48,880 --> 00:01:49,030
Yeah.

52
00:01:49,880 --> 00:01:54,690
They're pretty much mixed I think, but you will definitely hear busted if you're talking to native English speakers.

53
00:01:54,840 --> 00:01:56,950
Or even like, for example, your arm.

54
00:01:57,480 --> 00:01:58,230
He's got a busted arm.

55
00:01:58,800 --> 00:01:59,800
Exactly.

56
00:01:59,800 --> 00:01:59,930
So busted.

57
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:01,800
All right.

58
00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:03,510
So let's listen to our dialogue for the first time.

59
00:02:03,720 --> 00:02:08,090
Let's see what's going on with this car trouble and then we'll come back and explain some things.

60
00:02:08,880 --> 00:02:09,880
Howdy.

61
00:02:09,880 --> 00:02:10,880
Nice car.

62
00:02:10,880 --> 00:02:11,880
What seems to be the problem?

63
00:02:20,120 --> 00:02:24,660
I don't know.

64
00:02:25,040 --> 00:02:29,240
This stupid old car started spewing white smoke and it just died on me.

65
00:02:29,240 --> 00:02:31,360
Luckily I managed to start it up and drive it here.

66
00:02:32,060 --> 00:02:32,960
What do you think it is?

67
00:02:33,280 --> 00:02:33,980
Not sure yet.

68
00:02:34,460 --> 00:02:36,560
How about you pop the hood and we could take a look?

69
00:02:38,720 --> 00:02:39,880
Mm.

70
00:02:39,880 --> 00:02:43,040
It doesn't look good.

71
00:02:43,360 --> 00:02:43,940
What do you mean?

72
00:02:44,480 --> 00:02:46,540
My daddy gave me this car for my birthday last month.

73
00:02:46,740 --> 00:02:48,180
It's brand new.

74
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:52,140
Well, missy, the white smoke that you saw is steam from the radiator.

75
00:02:52,880 --> 00:02:56,640
You overheated your engine so now the pistons are busted, and so is your transmission.

76
00:02:57,520 --> 00:03:00,360
You should have called us and we could have towed you over here when your car died.

77
00:03:00,880 --> 00:03:04,110
Ugh. So, how long is this going to take?

78
00:03:04,110 --> 00:03:05,130
An hour?

79
00:03:05,880 --> 00:03:07,550
I'm afraid a bit more than that.

80
00:03:08,070 --> 00:03:14,790
We need to order the spare parts, take apart your electrical system, fuel pump, and engine, and then put it back together again.

81
00:03:15,330 --> 00:03:17,890
You're going to have to leave it here for at least, uh, two weeks.

82
00:03:18,570 --> 00:03:18,930
What?

83
00:03:19,650 --> 00:03:21,050
How am I supposed to go to school?

84
00:03:21,650 --> 00:03:22,170
Or go shopping?

85
00:03:22,970 --> 00:03:24,610
This is not happening.

86
00:03:29,610 --> 00:03:35,650
So if I were the mechanic, I would not be very happy to be dealing with someone like this girl.

87
00:03:36,330 --> 00:03:45,570
I think it's, uh, well maybe it's a little bit stereotypical that we put it as a girl, because guys and girls, so many people don't know anything about cars or mechanics.

88
00:03:45,880 --> 00:03:49,190
And in the States, there's a lot of female mechanics too, so it's, you know, it's mixed.

89
00:03:50,250 --> 00:03:56,880
But definitely it's something that happens all the time, and probably the mechanic will overcharge her as well, because apparently she doesn't know what she's doing.

90
00:03:57,550 --> 00:04:00,880
And that's one of the reasons we want to give you the vocabulary to deal with these situations.

91
00:04:01,370 --> 00:04:04,510
So let's look at some of those car words in today's Language Takeaway.

92
00:04:08,880 --> 00:04:14,880
Alright, so we're going to take a look at very specific words related to cars and the engines and everything.

93
00:04:14,980 --> 00:04:16,880
So let's start with the first one.

94
00:04:17,580 --> 00:04:17,880
Radiator.

95
00:04:17,880 --> 00:04:21,880
Radiator. Alright, so the radiator of the car.

96
00:04:22,730 --> 00:04:26,630
So a radiator is something that you normally find in the front of the car near the engine.

97
00:04:27,130 --> 00:04:28,880
It usually gets very, very hot, right?

98
00:04:28,880 --> 00:04:29,880
Yeah.

99
00:04:29,880 --> 00:04:33,880
So one of the big issues is radiators getting too hot because there's no way to cool them down.

100
00:04:33,880 --> 00:04:40,880
Mm-hmm. So if you live in a place that's very hot, the radiator keeps your engine cool because it has water in it.

101
00:04:41,290 --> 00:04:47,670
So if it overheats, or maybe you didn't put enough water in there, then it'll start spewing steam like in this dialogue.

102
00:04:47,890 --> 00:04:53,010
And if you have a problem with your radiator, you could actually do a lot of damage to your engine, which is why this is important.

103
00:04:53,170 --> 00:04:55,050
Exactly, and that's what happened in this dialogue.

104
00:04:55,490 --> 00:04:56,470
So a radiator.

105
00:04:57,090 --> 00:05:03,470
Now, because the radiator wasn't working well, it overheated the engine, and he said they had some busted pistons.

106
00:05:03,910 --> 00:05:05,210
So broken pistons.

107
00:05:05,450 --> 00:05:08,750
Pistons are important because they're kind of like valves that go up and down.

108
00:05:09,030 --> 00:05:09,210
Right.

109
00:05:09,410 --> 00:05:15,490
So inside your engine, you have mini explosions, and the pistons move up and down, and that's what actually moves your car.

110
00:05:15,990 --> 00:05:16,150
Right.

111
00:05:16,330 --> 00:05:25,880
And so pistons, in general, are things in mechanics. So like a trumpet has pistons, things that go up and down and that allow for the flow of air.

112
00:05:25,880 --> 00:05:27,850
Air or something like that. Very good.

113
00:05:28,170 --> 00:05:29,030
So that's a piston.

114
00:05:29,890 --> 00:05:33,590
And, well, another very common mechanic term is the transmission.

115
00:05:33,880 --> 00:05:35,820
Alright, so the transmission.

116
00:05:35,980 --> 00:05:37,640
What happens if your transmission dies?

117
00:05:38,080 --> 00:05:38,660
Can you still drive?

118
00:05:38,980 --> 00:05:42,880
You basically can't move, or maybe you only have one gear or two gears.

119
00:05:42,880 --> 00:05:47,240
Alright, so think about transmission as coming from the word to transmit. That means to transfer.

120
00:05:47,880 --> 00:05:54,360
So this is the part of the car that transfers energy or the power to actually move the car.

121
00:05:54,700 --> 00:05:55,900
So the transmission.

122
00:05:56,880 --> 00:06:01,430
And we've got the mechanic who says, well, we've got to order you some spare parts.

123
00:06:01,970 --> 00:06:03,690
So first, let's look at the word spare.

124
00:06:04,330 --> 00:06:05,210
What's another word for spare?

125
00:06:05,690 --> 00:06:07,650
Additional, maybe extra.

126
00:06:07,880 --> 00:06:17,880
Right. So spare means extra. Parts are, well, pieces. So he's talking about ordering a new transmission, new pistons, things to replace the broken things.

127
00:06:18,760 --> 00:06:19,440
Spare parts.

128
00:06:20,140 --> 00:06:24,140
So this is where you go to the mechanic to get some spare parts and get them installed.

129
00:06:24,500 --> 00:06:28,460
You probably don't have many spare parts at home, right? You got a transmission lying around.

130
00:06:29,280 --> 00:06:31,740
Well, yeah, so spare parts.

131
00:06:32,500 --> 00:06:35,720
And the last word that we're going to take a look at is a fuel pump.

132
00:06:36,320 --> 00:06:39,720
Okay, so a pump is, you could have a bike pump.

133
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:40,700
A water pump.

134
00:06:40,880 --> 00:06:41,380
Water pump.

135
00:06:41,540 --> 00:06:44,460
So these are something, these are some things that we use very, very often.

136
00:06:44,720 --> 00:06:48,720
But a fuel pump is something that allows gas, gasoline, to get into your engine.

137
00:06:49,440 --> 00:06:54,860
So it's basically a device that pushes gasoline into the engine so you can start.

138
00:06:55,200 --> 00:06:56,820
And that's the same thing with the water pump.

139
00:06:57,100 --> 00:06:58,600
It's a device that pushes water.

140
00:06:59,100 --> 00:07:01,880
Or an air pump is something that pushes air into your tires and your bicycle.

141
00:07:01,880 --> 00:07:05,880
Exactly. So a fuel pump, water pump, etc.

142
00:07:06,130 --> 00:07:07,610
Wow, I feel like we know a lot about cars.

143
00:07:07,880 --> 00:07:08,850
Yeah, apparently we do.

144
00:07:08,990 --> 00:07:17,930
See? Alright, so now that we've taken a look at all of these great words, let's move on to a couple of phrases in Fluency Builder.

145
00:07:21,090 --> 00:07:24,190
Alright, these are phrases that you hear sometimes when you're talking about electronics.

146
00:07:24,230 --> 00:07:27,710
But in today's lesson, we're also talking about specific car phrases.

147
00:07:27,880 --> 00:07:30,880
So you're going to hear these all the time at the mechanics.

148
00:07:30,880 --> 00:07:35,780
Right, and the first one is, the car suddenly died on me.

149
00:07:35,880 --> 00:07:37,320
Alright, to die on.

150
00:07:37,540 --> 00:07:39,720
So we keep these two words together, die on.

151
00:07:40,340 --> 00:07:43,800
Oh man, so while you were driving, your car died.

152
00:07:43,960 --> 00:07:44,780
It stopped moving.

153
00:07:45,680 --> 00:07:48,840
So that means that it doesn't work anymore.

154
00:07:49,360 --> 00:07:53,480
And it's not only for cars, right? You can say my MP3 player died on me today.

155
00:07:53,840 --> 00:07:59,320
Exactly, and what it means is, it's not like you left it and you forgot about it and you tried it again and it didn't work.

156
00:07:59,460 --> 00:08:01,520
It means while you're using it, it dies.

157
00:08:01,740 --> 00:08:02,300
It doesn't work.

158
00:08:02,880 --> 00:08:07,010
So I think this phrase is a good one to give some other examples.

159
00:08:07,170 --> 00:08:07,880
So let's listen.

160
00:08:33,880 --> 00:08:38,880
So this next one is a phrase you hear, the first thing you hear maybe, when you tell someone you have a car problem.

161
00:08:39,430 --> 00:08:41,090
Right, you tell them to pop the hood.

162
00:08:41,470 --> 00:08:42,670
Or let's pop the hood.

163
00:08:43,390 --> 00:08:49,690
So this means let's open the front part of the car, the hood, to look at the engine, transmission, all those parts.

164
00:08:50,150 --> 00:08:50,430
Exactly.

165
00:08:50,990 --> 00:08:54,880
So you can say open the hood, but the most common one is pop the hood.

166
00:08:55,750 --> 00:09:02,880
So the reason is because there is a little spring and you have to pull inside the car and it goes pop, and there's a little jump.

167
00:09:02,880 --> 00:09:03,880
Yeah, exactly.

168
00:09:04,680 --> 00:09:05,880
So it's literally popping.

169
00:09:06,600 --> 00:09:08,880
Right, or you can also say the trunk, right, the back part of the car.

170
00:09:09,860 --> 00:09:11,200
Hey, can you pop the trunk for me?

171
00:09:11,320 --> 00:09:11,880
I've got to put some stuff inside.

172
00:09:11,880 --> 00:09:14,880
Exactly, so pop the hood, pop the trunk.

173
00:09:15,680 --> 00:09:20,520
And next we've got this girl actually, who's talking about her car because she just got this car.

174
00:09:20,740 --> 00:09:23,140
So what's another way to say it's a new car?

175
00:09:23,540 --> 00:09:24,740
It's brand new.

176
00:09:25,140 --> 00:09:25,880
Is that more new than new?

177
00:09:25,880 --> 00:09:27,880
It's like newer, newer than new.

178
00:09:27,880 --> 00:09:36,880
Alright, so brand, you think about brands like Ford or Gucci, these are brands, but brand new means she maybe got it like today.

179
00:09:37,740 --> 00:09:40,660
So it's brand new, it's very, very new.

180
00:09:41,120 --> 00:09:41,880
Did you see my brand new watch?

181
00:09:42,720 --> 00:09:45,880
Oh, it is nice, did you get it? Alright, so brand new.

182
00:09:47,040 --> 00:09:50,880
And well, the last phrase that we have for you today is to take apart something.

183
00:09:52,020 --> 00:09:56,800
Alright, so this is very popular with young children, like they like to take things apart so they can find out how they work.

184
00:09:56,880 --> 00:09:59,880
But to take apart means to take all the pieces and to pull them out.

185
00:10:01,160 --> 00:10:02,820
Yeah, I used to do that a lot when I was a kid.

186
00:10:02,920 --> 00:10:10,080
I used to take apart any electronic device to kind of see how it works and then I would try to put it back together again.

187
00:10:10,360 --> 00:10:11,300
That's why you're so smart.

188
00:10:11,560 --> 00:10:14,060
Well, it never really worked, it worked half the time.

189
00:10:14,320 --> 00:10:16,880
So the problem is always putting them back together again, right?

190
00:10:16,880 --> 00:10:24,880
Exactly, so you can take it apart, but then you put it back together and there's always a screw missing or a screw that's extra or two or three parts.

191
00:10:25,080 --> 00:10:26,200
Not a good sign.

192
00:10:26,880 --> 00:10:33,380
Alright, so we've taken a look at a lot of great words and phrases, so let's listen to this dialogue for the last time.

193
00:10:44,710 --> 00:10:45,150
Howdy.

194
00:10:45,470 --> 00:10:46,130
Nice car.

195
00:10:46,450 --> 00:10:47,850
What seems to be the problem?

196
00:10:48,350 --> 00:10:53,050
I don't know. This stupid old car started spewing white smoke and it just died on me.

197
00:10:53,750 --> 00:10:55,830
Luckily, I managed to start it up and drive it here.

198
00:10:56,410 --> 00:10:57,410
What do you think it is?

199
00:10:57,710 --> 00:10:58,450
Not sure yet.

200
00:10:58,870 --> 00:11:00,880
How about you pop the hood and we can take a look?

201
00:11:05,880 --> 00:11:07,390
Mmm, doesn't look good.

202
00:11:07,880 --> 00:11:08,410
What do you mean?

203
00:11:09,050 --> 00:11:11,030
My daddy gave me this car for my birthday last month.

204
00:11:11,210 --> 00:11:12,610
It's brand new.

205
00:11:12,880 --> 00:11:16,880
Well, missy, the white smoke that you saw is steam from the radiator.

206
00:11:17,250 --> 00:11:21,050
You overheated your engine so now the pistons are busted and so is your transmission.

207
00:11:21,740 --> 00:11:24,840
You should have called us and we could have towed you over here when your car died.

208
00:11:24,880 --> 00:11:28,560
Ugh, so how long is this going to take?

209
00:11:28,560 --> 00:11:29,580
An hour?

210
00:11:30,220 --> 00:11:32,000
I'm afraid a bit more than that.

211
00:11:32,480 --> 00:11:39,260
We need to order the spare parts, take apart your electrical system, fuel pump and engine, and then put it back together again.

212
00:11:39,840 --> 00:11:42,360
You're going to have to leave it here for at least two weeks.

213
00:11:43,100 --> 00:11:43,400
What?

214
00:11:44,220 --> 00:11:45,500
How am I supposed to go to school?

215
00:11:45,500 --> 00:11:46,700
Or go shopping?

216
00:11:47,440 --> 00:11:49,080
This is not happening.

217
00:11:55,880 --> 00:12:02,880
Alright, so car trouble. Have you ever had any very serious car trouble or something weird happened to your car?

218
00:12:03,340 --> 00:12:12,660
Actually, the week before I decided to leave Chicago, I had this 1991 Honda Civic hatchback, so only two doors.

219
00:12:12,880 --> 00:12:19,880
And I was driving home from work, I was on the highway, had the radio on, the windows open, and then doo doo doo doo, boom.

220
00:12:19,880 --> 00:12:20,880
It died.

221
00:12:21,460 --> 00:12:23,780
It died in the middle of the highway during rush hour.

222
00:12:24,470 --> 00:12:26,880
So that's when there's tons of cars and I didn't know what to do.

223
00:12:26,880 --> 00:12:31,410
So I'm calling people and then all of a sudden I see someone tapping on my window.

224
00:12:31,410 --> 00:12:32,510
And there's cars everywhere.

225
00:12:33,170 --> 00:12:33,730
And it's this guy.

226
00:12:34,530 --> 00:12:36,880
And he's like, listen, do you need a hand? I said yes.

227
00:12:37,630 --> 00:12:44,880
So I put the car in neutral so that there's no brake. And he pushed it to a side road and gave me a ride home.

228
00:12:44,880 --> 00:12:45,880
No way.

229
00:12:45,880 --> 00:12:47,770
He was the coolest guy, he was so nice.

230
00:12:49,300 --> 00:12:52,880
And he was just there with his truck, he's a gardener, and just gave me a ride home.

231
00:12:52,880 --> 00:12:53,880
It was super, super cool.

232
00:12:53,880 --> 00:12:54,880
Wow.

233
00:12:54,980 --> 00:13:00,440
And so it turns out my engine died. No, the engine was new, it was the transmission, it was shot.

234
00:13:00,880 --> 00:13:01,760
It was totally broken.

235
00:13:01,880 --> 00:13:02,880
Wow.

236
00:13:02,880 --> 00:13:04,880
But I was very lucky to have such a kind person.

237
00:13:05,040 --> 00:13:14,780
Yeah, that's a good story because usually people are too afraid or maybe a little bit reluctant to stop and see if you're okay or if you need a ride or something.

238
00:13:14,880 --> 00:13:17,460
Because you can't really trust people these days.

239
00:13:18,450 --> 00:13:19,170
But it's good.

240
00:13:19,510 --> 00:13:24,210
Once I saw somebody on the side of the road that had a flat tire, it was two girls and they didn't know what to do.

241
00:13:24,390 --> 00:13:25,350
So I was passing by.

242
00:13:25,810 --> 00:13:26,770
It was actually raining.

243
00:13:27,490 --> 00:13:28,850
And I was like, hey, do you guys need a hand?

244
00:13:29,010 --> 00:13:29,790
And they're like, yeah, please.

245
00:13:30,090 --> 00:13:33,010
We can't locate my dad or whatever.

246
00:13:33,030 --> 00:13:35,530
So I was like, changing the tire, it's not a big deal, right?

247
00:13:35,880 --> 00:13:38,880
And they wanted to tip me, they wanted to give me some money.

248
00:13:38,880 --> 00:13:39,880
Are you serious?

249
00:13:39,880 --> 00:13:41,910
They're like, oh, please take this, you're so kind, your time, and you're all wet.

250
00:13:42,070 --> 00:13:43,410
And I'm like, no, no, don't worry about it.

251
00:13:43,880 --> 00:13:44,630
No, it's just a nice thing to do.

252
00:13:45,050 --> 00:13:48,490
If you know anything about cars, I mean, or if you can offer someone a lift.

253
00:13:48,880 --> 00:13:53,880
I know it's not terribly safe to ride with strangers, but this guy totally helped me out.

254
00:13:53,880 --> 00:14:01,390
